The retry loop pattern, made concrete for both a counter and a stack push:

```java
// Lock-free counter — no lock, just retry on CAS failure
AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);
int old, next;
do {
    old = counter.get();
    next = old + 1;
} while (!counter.compareAndSet(old, next));

// Lock-free stack push — same shape, on a linked node
Node newNode = new Node(value);
Node oldTop;
do {
    oldTop = top.get();
    newNode.next = oldTop;
} while (!top.compareAndSet(oldTop, newNode));
```

Both follow the identical shape: read, compute, CAS, retry on failure. Under low contention this loop almost never retries — it's essentially free. Under high contention, many threads can retry repeatedly, which is the lock-free trade made concrete: no thread ever *blocks*, but a thread can still do wasted, retried work under contention.
